findings.message:
Menu/key actions spawn a new thread per click instead of submitting to a serializing executor: The diff replaces `uiRun(this::pull)` with `this.context?.guiContext?.addMenuAction("Pull") { kotlin.concurrent.thread { this.pull() } }` (and the same pattern at lines 45, 50 and 54 for Push and both key bindings). This contradicts the stated design of a shared single-thread `backgroundExecutor`: every menu click or key press creates a brand-new thread, so rapid activations still run `pull()`/`push()` concurrently and can interleave repository reads/writes and project mutations — exactly the corruption case this PR claims to fix. Additionally `kotlin.concurrent.thread` defaults to `isDaemon = false`, so the hung/blocked worker also prevents JVM shutdown, contrary to the description's daemon-thread rationale.
Scenario: A user double-clicks the Pull menu item (or presses ctrl+BACK_SLASH twice quickly). Two independent threads enter pull(), both call readLocalRepository()/projectToLocalRepository()/writeLocalRepository() on the same on-disk repository, and interleaved writes leave the local repository in a partially updated or inconsistent state.
Create one shared executor and submit all four entry points to it, e.g.:

private val backgroundExecutor: ExecutorService = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor { r ->
    Thread(r, "$ID-background").apply { isDaemon = true }
}

this.context?.guiContext?.addMenuAction("Pull") { backgroundExecutor.submit(this::pull) }
this.context?.guiContext?.addMenuAction("Push") { backgroundExecutor.submit(this::push) }

and use the same submission for the two registerGlobalKeyBinding callbacks.
